f 17592186045417] [17592186419390 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419390 #edb/ref 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a,b\\in Z,$ if $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2),$ then $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na={{field-6}},\\,b={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n0{{field-8}}2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,\\text{and}\\,0{{field-9}}2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).\n$$\n\nSo, the pair $a=$ {{field-10}} and $b=$ {{field-11}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419390 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02cf0cdd-dd4e-5a78-a0c4-272aec9af99d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419390 #edb/ref 1074 "By denoting $P(a,b)$ and $Q(a,b)$ as\n\n- $P(a,b):\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$\n\n- $Q(a,b):\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a,b\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a,b\\in Z,\\,(a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2))\\,∧\\,(a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exist integers $a$ and $b$ such that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,\\text{and}\\,a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $(a,b)=(0,2)$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose\n\n$$\na=0,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nTo check that this is a required counterexample, we proceed as follows:\n\nHence, we have that \n\n$$\n0≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,\\text{and}\\,0≢2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, the pair $a=0$ and $b=2$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419391 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419391 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419391 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221959" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419391 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419391 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following false statement.\n\nIf $a<b$ then $3a-1<2b+10$ for any $a\\in Z.$\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419391 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5678787f-ddbb-5c28-abd5-08d5ea9f1942" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419391 #edb/ref 1074 "By denoting $P(a)$ and $Q(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a):\\,a<b$\n\n- $Q(a):\\,3a-1<2b+10$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nA *counterexample* is a particular case that disproves the statement. Equivalently, it's a case that proves the negation is true.\n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(a<b)\\,∧\\,(3a-1\\ge 2b+10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\na<b\\,\\text{and}\\,3a-1\\ge 2b+10.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419392 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419392 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419392 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419392 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419392 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419392 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419392 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419392 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419392 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419392 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419392 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419392 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221742" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419392 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419392 #edb/ref 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a\\in Z,$ if $10∣a$ and $15∣a,$ then $150∣a.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nLet's choose $a=$ {{field-7}}.\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n10{{field-8}}30\\,\\text{and}\\,15{{field-9}}30\\,\\text{and}\\,150{{field-10}}30.\n$$\n\nSo, $a=$ {{field-11}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419392 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d1cc0610-ee4b-58b1-8b76-c910ff50a754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419392 #edb/ref 1074 "By denoting $P(a),$ $Q(a),$ and $R(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a):\\,10∣a$\n\n- $Q(a):\\,15∣a$\n\n- $R(a):\\,150∣a$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a\\in Z,\\,(P∧Q)⇒R.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,(P∧Q)⇒R) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬((P∧Q)⇒R) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬(P∧Q)∨R) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(P∧Q)∧¬R \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧Q∧¬R \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(10∣a)\\,∧\\,(15∣a)\\,∧\\,(150∤a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n10∣a\\,\\text{and}\\,15∣a\\,\\text{and}\\,150∤a.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $a=30$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose $a=30.$\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n10∣30\\,\\text{and}\\,15∣30\\,\\text{and}\\,150∤30.\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $a=30$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419393 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419393 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419393 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221724" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419393 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419393 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following false statement.\n\nIf $3∣a,$ then $6∣a$ for any $a\\in Z.$\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419393 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d7920e3b-ba93-57c2-9dd2-0e80e521ff6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419393 #edb/ref 1074 "By denoting $P(a)$ and $Q(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a):\\,3∣a$\n\n- $Q(a):\\,6∣a$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nA *counterexample* is a particular case that disproves the statement. Equivalently, it's a case that proves the negation is true.\n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(3∣a)\\,∧\\,(6∤a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n3∣a\\,\\text{and}\\,6∤a.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
